From: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] mt76: dma: skip lock+MMIO read in tx_cleanup for an already-empty queue

mt76_dma_tx_cleanup(dev, q, false) unconditionally takes q->cleanup_lock
and does an uncached MMIO register read (mt76_dma_read_dma_idx(), which
resolves to Q_READ(q, dma_idx) -> readl()) before the while-loop even
checks whether q->queued is nonzero. For an already-empty queue this
lock+MMIO cost is pure overhead: the loop condition is `q->queued > 0 &&
q->tail != last`, so an empty queue does zero work regardless of what
`last` reads as.

This is not a rare case on the MT7622 SoC-internal MT7615/WMAC (2.4GHz)
radio, which has no WED hardware DMA offload (unlike the PCIe MT7915
5GHz path): every TX-status notify event on this chip
(mt7615_mac_tx_free(), mt7615/mac.c) unconditionally calls
mt76_queue_tx_cleanup(..., false) once for the PSD queue plus once per
WMM AC (5 calls total on the non-is_mt7615() MT7622-WMAC branch, versus
2 calls in mt7915's equivalent handler) - on a dual-core MT7622 SoC
where this IRQ already shares its one CPU with the 5GHz radio's MCU/
status work and all software-forwarded Ethernet TX (see this fork's
IRQ-affinity fix in files/etc/rc.local). Most of those 5 calls hit a
queue with nothing queued at that moment, each still paying a spinlock
acquisition and an MMIO round-trip for no result.

Add an early return for the non-flush, empty-queue case before the lock
is taken. flush=true is deliberately excluded: its post-loop
mt76_dma_sync_idx()/mt76_dma_kick_queue() calls resync and kick the ring
unconditionally and callers (queue teardown/reset paths) rely on that
happening even when nothing was queued, so flush must keep falling
through exactly as before. This is the shared core DMA path
(drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/dma.c), so every mt76 driver that
calls tx_cleanup from a hot per-event path with flush=false benefits
(mt7615, mt7603, mt76x02, mt7915's MCU cleanup, mt7921, mt7925), not
just this board's 2.4GHz radio.

Verified: `dma.o` compiles clean under this package's own `-Werror`
build flags against the configured 6.12.94 kernel tree (out-of-tree
module build, cross-toolchain). A full module link was not attempted in
this session (would require replicating the OpenWrt mac80211-backports
include environment beyond a bare `make M=` invocation); the change
itself only reorders an early return using already-in-scope parameters
and touches no external symbol, so this is a low-risk, behavior-
preserving change to verify further as part of a normal package build.
Not hardware-validated - no live E8450 access was available this
session; a live A/B (mpstat/perf under saturating 2.4GHz traffic) is
recommended before treating the measured CPU savings as proven, though
correctness (an empty queue has nothing to clean up) is provable by
inspection alone.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
---
--- a/dma.c
+++ b/dma.c
@@ -428,6 +428,15 @@
 	if (!q || !q->ndesc)
 		return;
 
+	/* Non-flush cleanup is opportunistic: an empty queue has nothing to
+	 * reap regardless of the hardware DMA index, so skip the lock and
+	 * the uncached MMIO register read entirely. flush=true must still
+	 * fall through unconditionally: it also resyncs/kicks the ring
+	 * below, which callers rely on even when nothing was queued.
+	 */
+	if (!flush && !q->queued)
+		return;
+
 	spin_lock_bh(&q->cleanup_lock);
 	if (flush)
 		last = -1;
